WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.729
Rosa, an electrician, injures her shoulder and can no longer climb or pull wire.

00:00:05.729 --> 00:00:10.639
She could manage a hardware store or estimate jobs from a desk.

00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:11.866
Is she disabled?

00:00:11.866 --> 00:00:16.367
The answer depends entirely on how her policy defines the word.

00:00:16.367 --> 00:00:21.687
Disability income insurance does not pay because a person is hurt; it pays

00:00:21.687 --> 00:00:26.597
because the person meets the policy’s definition of total or partial disability.

00:00:26.597 --> 00:00:29.870
Two policies with identical benefit amounts can produce

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:32.735
opposite claim decisions on the same injury.

00:00:32.735 --> 00:00:36.417
Before quoting any disability policy, a producer reads the

00:00:36.417 --> 00:00:40.100
definition and explains it in the client’s own terms.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:44.997
An own-occupation definition pays when the insured cannot perform the material and substantial

00:00:44.997 --> 00:00:49.893
duties of her regular occupation, even if she could work at something else.

00:00:49.893 --> 00:00:53.660
Under a true own-occupation policy Rosa is totally disabled the

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:57.427
day she cannot work as an electrician, and she may

00:00:57.427 --> 00:01:00.817
earn income in another job while collecting full benefits.

00:01:00.817 --> 00:01:05.713
A modified own-occupation definition pays only if she is also not working elsewhere.

00:01:05.713 --> 00:01:09.857
Own-occupation coverage is the most favorable and the most expensive, and

00:01:09.857 --> 00:01:14.000
insurers reserve it for professionals and skilled trades with definable duties.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.363
An any-occupation definition pays only when the insured cannot perform the duties of

00:01:18.363 --> 00:01:22.726
any occupation for which she is reasonably suited by education, training and experience.

00:01:22.726 --> 00:01:27.425
Rosa, able to manage a store, would not be totally disabled under this test.

00:01:27.425 --> 00:01:32.124
It is the strictest definition, resembles the Social Security standard, and costs the least.

00:01:32.124 --> 00:01:35.816
Many policies and most group long-term disability plans use a split

00:01:35.816 --> 00:01:39.508
definition: own occupation for the first two years, then any occupation.

00:01:39.508 --> 00:01:42.864
A producer must warn clients that benefits can end at

00:01:42.864 --> 00:01:46.220
the two-year mark if they could do other suitable work.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:48.884
A third approach drops the occupational test.

00:01:48.884 --> 00:01:52.308
A pure loss of income, or income replacement, contract

00:01:52.308 --> 00:01:55.352
pays based on what the insured actually loses.

00:01:55.352 --> 00:01:59.538
If Rosa’s shoulder cuts her income from 6,000 dollars a month

00:01:59.538 --> 00:02:03.723
to 3,000 dollars, the policy pays a benefit proportional to the

00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:07.529
50 percent loss, whether she stopped working or changed jobs.

00:02:07.529 --> 00:02:10.953
These contracts need no definition of total disability for

00:02:10.953 --> 00:02:14.378
most claims, because they measure dollars rather than duties.

00:02:14.378 --> 00:02:17.422
They suit self-employed people whose income varies and

00:02:17.422 --> 00:02:20.466
whose disability may be partial from the start.

00:02:20.466 --> 00:02:23.510
Lesson 30 shows the residual arithmetic in detail.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:26.181
Some losses are so severe that the policy

00:02:26.181 --> 00:02:28.851
presumes total disability without applying the occupational test.

00:02:28.851 --> 00:02:32.857
The usual list is total and permanent loss of sight in both

00:02:32.857 --> 00:02:36.863
eyes, hearing in both ears, speech, or the use of two limbs.

00:02:36.863 --> 00:02:40.868
If Rosa loses both hands in an accident she is presumed totally

00:02:40.868 --> 00:02:44.874
disabled, benefits begin, often without the elimination period, and they continue for

00:02:44.874 --> 00:02:48.212
the full benefit period even if she later earns income.

00:02:48.212 --> 00:02:52.218
Presumptive disability is a floor that guarantees payment in the worst cases;

00:02:52.218 --> 00:02:55.890
everything short of the list still runs through the policy definition.

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:59.968
Policies require the insured to be under the regular care

00:02:59.968 --> 00:03:03.639
of a physician for the condition causing the disability.

00:03:03.639 --> 00:03:08.125
The requirement prevents claims for conditions nobody is treating and lets

00:03:08.125 --> 00:03:12.612
the insurer verify continuing disability through medical records and periodic proof.

00:03:12.612 --> 00:03:17.098
Most modern policies waive it when care would not improve the

00:03:17.098 --> 00:03:21.177
condition, so a paralyzed insured need not schedule pointless visits.

00:03:21.177 --> 00:03:24.847
Rosa must keep her orthopedic appointments while she claims,

00:03:24.847 --> 00:03:28.518
and her physician’s reports will support each monthly benefit.

00:03:28.518 --> 00:03:33.820
Put the pieces together: definition met, care maintained, elimination period served, benefit paid.
